# ============================================================================
# OnyxAgent — environment template. Copy to .env (never commit .env).
# Everything is OPTIONAL: the app is client-first (BYOK lives in the browser
# vault); these vars are the server-side baseline + operator conveniences.
# Deploying to Vercel instead? The one-click template (every key below is
# prompted, DDG_FORWARD_CLIENT_IP prefilled) lives in DEPLOY.md.
# ============================================================================

# ── Durable sandbox browser (the browser_startup_failed fix) ────────────────
# Build the custom template ONCE (bakes cloakbrowser + the ~200MB stealth
# Chromium + Xvfb into the image) and every sandbox then boots the browser
# in SECONDS instead of pip-installing + downloading at runtime (~6-10 min):
#
#   bash scripts/build-browser-sandbox-template.sh
#
# E2B_TEMPLATE_ID=onyx-cloakbrowser

# ── Sandboxes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-side fallback E2B key (users normally bring their own in Settings).
# ALSO the DuckDuckGo enabler for image/video search: with a key present
# (this one or the user's BYOK key), the DDG vqd → i.js/v.js handshake runs
# INSIDE the acquired E2B sandbox, so duckduckgo.com sees the SANDBOX's IP —
# the egress its WAF serves — instead of the deployment's blocked one
# (provider reports "ddg"). Without any E2B key, Miklium answers.
# E2B_API_KEY=

# ── Database (server-side task/schedule/cloud-key store) ─────────────────────
# Defaults to db/custom.db (SQLite) when unset.
# DATABASE_URL=file:./db/custom.db

# ── Background tasks (Trigger.dev Cloud direct execution) ────────────────────
# Public origin of THIS deployment — the cloud-fired schedule tick
# (/api/tasks/schedules/tick) and the worker deploy need it. Set it on the
# app server when deploying behind a proxy. NOTE: background agent runs use
# each USER'S own provider credentials (BYOK) — there is no app-side LLM
# endpoint to expose, so this is NOT an LLM bridge URL anymore.
# APP_URL=https://your-app.example.com

# Operator convenience: connect a key from Settings → Cloud instead of this.
# TRIGGER_CLOUD_KEY=
# TRIGGER_CLOUD_TASK_SLUG=agent-run
# TRIGGER_CLOUD_API_URL=https://api.trigger.dev

# AES master key for sealing the Trigger.dev cloud key + worker token at
# rest (defaults to a generated db/cloud-config.key when unset).
# TASKS_CLOUD_MASTER_KEY=

# Auto worker deploy (Settings → Cloud → Deploy runs this itself):
# TRIGGER_PROJECT_REF=
# TRIGGER_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── Misc tool keys (server-side fallbacks) ────────────────────────────────────
# TAVILY_API_KEY=

# ── Search egress ("no datacenter IP involved") ──────────────────────────────
# DuckDuckGo's WAF hard-blocks shared datacenter egress IPs (the image/video
# outage of 2026-11-01; region-hopping across iad1/sin1/hkg1/fra1 changed
# nothing), and no npm package escapes it — the block is on the EGRESS IP,
# not the client. Knobs that change where DDG traffic leaves from, in the
# route's priority order:
#
# 0) E2B_API_KEY (or the user's own sandbox key in Settings) — THE fix: the
#    whole DDG handshake executes inside the acquired E2B sandbox
#    (src/lib/search/ddg-e2b.ts) on the sandbox's own IP. Nothing to
#    deploy beyond the key you already use for code execution.
#
# 1) DDG_PROXY_BASE — route every DDG call (token page, i.js, v.js) through a
#    relay YOU control whose IP is not a blocked egress: a box at home, a
#    Tailscale exit node, or any always-on machine (live-verified 2026-11-01:
#    DDG's block targets hammered serverless egress like Vercel's, NOT every
#    datacenter range — a plain cloud-VM IP served 90+ results).
#    DDG then serves real results (provider stays "ddg").
#
#    TURNKEY RELAY — no nginx needed, one file, zero deps (Node 18+). Copy
#    scripts/ddg-relay.mjs to the relay box and run it:
#
#      PORT=8787 DDG_RELAY_TOKEN=<random-string> node ddg-relay.mjs
#      # listening on 0.0.0.0:8787 -> https://duckduckgo.com
#
#    Then set on the app (Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables):
#
#      DDG_PROXY_BASE=http(s)://<relay-host>:8787
#      DDG_RELAY_TOKEN=<same random-string>     # optional shared secret
#
#    The relay only ever proxies "/", "/i.js", "/v.js" (path allowlist —
#    not an open proxy), passes browser-grade + visitor-IP headers through,
#    and relays upstream statuses untouched so the app's soft-block detection
#    keeps working. Or use your own nginx instead if you prefer:
#
#      location /ddg/ {
#          proxy_pass https://duckduckgo.com/;
#          proxy_set_header Host duckduckgo.com;
#          proxy_ssl_server_name on;
#      }
#
#    DDG_PROXY_BASE=https://home.example.com/ddg
#
# 2) DDG_FORWARD_CLIENT_IP — forward each visitor's public IP on DDG calls
#    with the standard X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP / Forwarded headers (what
#    a transparent proxy does). Default ON; set to "false" to disable.
#    Honesty note: DDG's own WAF ignores these headers today (verified live
#    2026-11-01), so this is standard-correct semantics — not a block-bypass;
#    DDG_PROXY_BASE is the knob that actually removes the datacenter IP.
#    When both DDG and the relay are unreachable, the Miklium rescue keeps
#    image/video search working (provider honestly reports "miklium").
#
# DDG_PROXY_BASE=
# DDG_RELAY_TOKEN=
# DDG_FORWARD_CLIENT_IP=true
